# Solution — GIAM 4.3 Problem 5

## Problem

There is a set $ X $ such that, for all sets $ A $, we have $ X\,\triangle\,A = A $. What is $ X $?

## Answer

$$$
\boxed{\;X = \varnothing\;}
$$$

Confirmed for every subset of a five-element universe, and — as shown below — it is the only set with this property.

## Verification

Recall that $ X\,\triangle\,A $ consists of the elements lying in exactly one of $ X $ and $ A $. Taking $ X=\varnothing $:

$$$
\begin{aligned}
x\in\varnothing\,\triangle\,A
 &\iff \left(x\in\varnothing \land x\notin A\right) \lor \left(x\in A \land x\notin\varnothing\right) && \triangle \\
 &\iff \mathbf{c} \lor \left(x\in A \land \mathbf{t}\right) && \text{nothing lies in } \varnothing \\
 &\iff x\in A && \text{identity laws}
\end{aligned}
$$$

The first disjunct dies because $ x\in\varnothing $ is false; the clause $ x\notin\varnothing $ in the second is always true and drops out. $ \blacksquare $

Equivalently and more briefly, $ \varnothing\,\triangle\,A = \left(\varnothing\cup A\right)\setminus\left(\varnothing\cap A\right) = A\setminus\varnothing = A $, using the form proved in 4.3 Problem 3c.

## Uniqueness

The problem says “there **is** a set $ X $”, but in fact there is exactly one, and finding it takes a single substitution.

> **Claim.** If $ X\,\triangle\,A = A $ holds for all sets $ A $, then $ X = \varnothing $.
>
> **Proof.** The hypothesis holds for *every* $ A $, so in particular for $ A=\varnothing $. That instance reads
>
> $ X\,\triangle\,\varnothing = \varnothing . $
>
> But $ X\,\triangle\,\varnothing = X $ by the computation above with the roles reversed. Hence $ X = \varnothing $. $ \blacksquare $

Exhaustive search over the $ 32 $ subsets of a five-element universe returns exactly one $ X $ satisfying the condition, namely $ \varnothing $ — matching the proof.

## The Picture

The two panels below give the same fact twice: once as regions, once as bits.

