solutions for one of them.")

## Remark — This Is the Identity Element of a Group

The condition in the problem — an element that leaves everything unchanged — is precisely the definition of an **identity element** for the operation $ \triangle $. The remark at the end of 4.3 Problem 1 observed that $ \left(\mathcal{P}(U),\ \triangle\right) $ is an abelian group; this problem is asking for the identity of that group, and the answer is $ \varnothing $.

The uniqueness argument above is a special case of the standard group-theoretic one. If $ e $ and $ e' $ are both identities then

$$$
e = e\,\triangle\,e' = e' ,
$$$

the first equality because $ e' $ is an identity and the second because $ e $ is. So no operation has two identities, and the phrase “**the** identity” is justified in advance of knowing what it is.

The full group data for $ \triangle $, all verified over the $ 32 $ subsets:

$$$
\begin{array}{l|l}
\text{identity} & \varnothing \\
\text{inverse of } A & A \text{ itself, since } A\,\triangle\,A = \varnothing \\
\text{associative} & \text{yes} \\
\text{commutative} & \text{yes}
\end{array}
$$$

## Remark — Union and Intersection Have Identities Too, but Not Inverses

It is worth noticing that the question could have been asked of $ \cup $ or $ \cap $ and would still have had an answer:

$$$
\varnothing\cup A = A \quad\text{for all } A , \qquad U\cap A = A \quad\text{for all } A .
$$$

Both confirmed by exhaustive search, which also returns these as the unique identities. So $ \varnothing $ is the identity for two of the three operations and $ U $ for the third — and note that the answer to this problem would have been $ U $, not $ \varnothing $, had the operation been $ \cap $.

What separates $ \triangle $ from the other two is not the identity but the **inverses**. For $ \cup $, once an element has been added it can never be removed: $ A\cup X \supseteq A $ always, so $ A\cup X=\varnothing $ is impossible unless $ A $ is already empty. Verified: no set other than $ \varnothing $ has a $ \cup $-inverse, and no set other than $ U $ has a $ \cap $-inverse. Union and intersection only ever move in one direction, while $ \triangle $ can undo itself.

## Remark — Why Having an Identity and Inverses Matters

An identity element is not a curiosity; together with inverses it makes **equations solvable**. Consider

$$$
X\,\triangle\,A = B .
$$$

Symmetric-differencing both sides by $ A $ and using $ A\,\triangle\,A=\varnothing $ together with $ \varnothing\,\triangle\,X = X $ gives $ X = A\,\triangle\,B $, and this is the unique solution. Exhaustive check over all $ 32\times32 = 1024 $ pairs $ (A,B) $: **every** pair has exactly one solution, always equal to $ A\,\triangle\,B $.

The contrast with the other two operations is stark. For the same $ 1024 $ pairs:

$$$
\begin{array}{l|r|r|r}
\text{number of solutions } X & 0 & 1 & \text{more than } 1 \\ \hline
X\,\triangle\,A = B & 0 & 1024 & 0 \\
X\cup A = B & 781 & 32 & 211 \\
X\cap A = B & 781 & 32 & 211
\end{array}
$$$

So more than three quarters of the time the equation $ X\cup A = B $ has no solution at all, and when it does have one it may have as many as $ 32 $. Identical figures for $ \cap $, which is no accident — the duality of 4.3 Problem 3 carries each $ \cup $-equation to a $ \cap $-equation.

This is exactly the practical difference between an operation that merely accumulates and one that is reversible, and it is the reason $ \triangle $ rather than $ \cup $ plays the role of addition in the Boolean ring $ \left(\mathcal{P}(U),\ \triangle,\ \cap\right) $.

## Remark — The Bitstring Reading

Fixing an order on $ U $ and recording each set by its membership bits, $ \triangle $ becomes bitwise XOR. In that language the problem asks for the bit vector $ X $ with

$$$
X \oplus A = A \quad\text{for every } A ,
$$$

and the answer is the all-zeros vector, which is the empty set:

$$$
\begin{array}{r|l}
A & 11010 \\
\varnothing & 00000 \\ \hline
A\oplus\varnothing & 11010
\end{array}
$$$

The reason is transparent at the level of a single bit: XOR with $ 0 $ leaves a bit alone, and XOR with $ 1 $ flips it. A set therefore acts on $ A $ by toggling exactly the positions it occupies, so the only set that toggles nothing is the one occupying no positions.

This also explains the self-inverse property at a glance — toggling the same positions twice returns to the start — and recovers 4.3 Problem 1's identification of $ \left(\mathcal{P}(U),\triangle\right) $ with $ \left(\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}\right)^{|U|} $, where the identity is of course the zero vector.
